---
import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
import PrincipleSpread from '@/components/modelling/PrincipleSpread.astro';
import ContrastPair from '@/components/modelling/ContrastPair.astro';
import ChapterEnd from '@/components/modelling/ChapterEnd.astro';
---

<ModellingLayout title="The context-first modelling manifesto" description="Start with the work. Make identity explicit. Connect meanings through accountable agreements. This is the modelling discipline we are applying to the Smart Property Data Trust Framework." chapter="Apply the method" claim="method" hideFooter>
  <p>
    A framework should make expertise more usable, not replace it with a vocabulary
    designed at a distance. Our approach begins with the questions a community needs
    to answer and the distinctions its work depends on. We then make those distinctions
    precise, challenge them with formal analysis, reuse suitable standards and connect
    them through explicit boundaries.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is a position about the order of discovery and the ownership of meaning.
    It is not an argument against ontological rigour, shared standards or common
    concepts. It rejects premature universality: assuming the same word means the
    same thing everywhere, or that placing every class in one hierarchy establishes
    a shared understanding.
  </p>

  <PrincipleSpread number={1} id="start-with-work" aliases={["questions","cycle","evidence","untrusted-evidence"]} title="Start with a question people need to answer">
    <p>Begin with a decision, exchange or misunderstanding in real work. Name the question, the evidence available and examples that would change the answer. A source form is valuable evidence, but its sections and fields are not automatically the conceptual structure of the domain.</p>
    <p>For Harbour Court, “which report describes the inspection of Flat 1?” immediately separates a dwelling, an event and a document version. Those distinctions earn their place by making the question answerable.</p>
    <Fragment slot="practice"><p>Write a competency question in ordinary language. Add at least one ordinary case and one counterexample. Ask who can judge the answer before choosing the encoding.</p><p><a href="/semantic-modelling/method/from-question-to-candidate">From question to candidate →</a></p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="misreading"><p>Evidence-led does not mean copying every source structure. The first-principles Property Pack approach in OPDA ADR-0067 treats sources as evidence to evaluate, not a schema that dictates the ontology.</p></Fragment>
  </PrincipleSpread>

  <PrincipleSpread number={2} id="own-meaning" aliases={["local-authority","responsibilities","participation"]} title="Give meaning an accountable home">
    <p>A context establishes where a meaning is coherent and who is responsible for changing it. It is not simply a department, software service or search category. A profession can participate in several contexts; a term can be classified under several subjects while retaining one semantic home.</p>
    <p>Local ownership prevents a convenient enterprise label from erasing an operational distinction. It also creates obligations: publish the definition, expose its assumptions, review change and explain what is promised at the boundary.</p>
    <Fragment slot="practice"><p>Name the context, the questions it serves and the authority for its meanings. Record the boundary agreement separately from particular term correspondences.</p><p><a href="/semantic-modelling/method/context-map-records">Strategic context maps →</a></p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="misreading"><p>Context-first is not “every team has its own truth”. Conflicting claims about the same event still require evidence. A context is a responsibility for meaning, not an exemption from consistency.</p></Fragment>
  </PrincipleSpread>

  <PrincipleSpread number={3} id="identity-before-equivalence" aliases={["distinctions","foundations"]} title="Establish identity before asserting equivalence">
    <p>A shared address can help locate records without making a building, dwelling and title record identical. Ask what an individual is, what distinguishes it from others and what changes it can survive. A label match or common source key cannot answer those questions alone.</p>
    <p>Use kinds, roles and phases deliberately. A person participating as buyer is not a new person. A state change is not automatically a new identity. A subdivision is not automatically a phase: the identity criterion must be resolved by the domain.</p>
    <Fragment slot="practice"><p>Test identity against corrections, concurrent roles, split and merge cases. Refuse an equivalence when the referents differ; keep an unresolved criterion visible.</p><p><a href="/semantic-modelling/method/roles-and-phases">Roles and phases →</a></p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="misreading"><p>UFO-informed distinctions and OntoClean analysis are required analytical disciplines in the selected source method. ODR-0118 prohibits domain-class subsumption under external upper ontologies within its scope. Analytical rigour is not a requirement to import an external class hierarchy.</p></Fragment>
  </PrincipleSpread>

  <PrincipleSpread number={4} id="connect-with-evidence" aliases={["connect-without-collapse"]} title="Connect meanings without disguising differences">
    <p>Reuse an externally governed term when its meaning fits. Where local meanings must remain distinct, record the relationship and the evidence for its strength. An exact conceptual correspondence, a close correspondence, a broader concept and identity of individuals are different commitments.</p>
    <p>A strategic context agreement says how communities depend on one another. A qualified mapping record says how particular terms correspond. Neither can stand in for the other. Each bridge must identify the individual mappings that actually apply.</p>
    <Fragment slot="practice"><p>Record the source and target versions, mapping predicate, matching-process justification, mapping date and mapping-set release. Preserve the SKOS statement and the required SSSOM/SEMAPV qualification.</p><p><a href="/semantic-modelling/method/mapping-records">Qualified term mappings →</a></p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="misreading"><p>“Connected” does not mean “equivalent”. Refusing a proposed mapping is a useful outcome. A confidence score cannot repair incompatible identity criteria.</p></Fragment>
  </PrincipleSpread>

  <PrincipleSpread number={5} id="respect-semantics" aliases={["reuse","separate-concerns"]} title="Keep meaning, checks and delivery distinct">
    <p>RDF statements, RDFS implications, applicability hints, SHACL constraints and materialisation rules do different jobs. Their composition must be explicit. Switching off a reasoner does not change the meaning of an exported RDFS domain declaration.</p>
    <p>A JSON schema, form or API is a delivery view of selected meaning, not a replacement for the model. Identify which relationships, qualifications and constraints survive the projection and which are omitted. A successful serialisation is not proof of semantic preservation.</p>
    <Fragment slot="practice"><p>Choose a bounded language profile. Supply positive and negative data examples, explicit competency queries and evidence for each claimed projection. Keep asserted and derived information distinguishable.</p><p><a href="/semantic-modelling/method/meaning-checks-and-delivery">Meaning, checks and delivery →</a></p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="misreading"><p>This is neither unrestricted OWL reasoning nor a claim that inference is forbidden. The selected method bounds its OWL surface and declares supported rule materialisation separately.</p></Fragment>
  </PrincipleSpread>

  <PrincipleSpread number={6} id="explain-the-boundary" title="Make evidence, time and limits part of the model">
    <p>A useful assertion can be traced to what supports it. A useful date says which event or period it concerns. A useful sensitivity classification tells readers what it describes without pretending to grant access. These concerns are part of trustworthy interpretation, not decorative metadata added after the model is finished.</p>
    <p>Keep the scope proportional. The eight selected ODR-0071 concerns require assessment, not eight separate ontologies. Reuse a shared artefact or record why a concern is not applicable when that is the justified disposition.</p>
    <Fragment slot="practice"><p>Distinguish event time from recording time, resource provenance from claim provenance, and classification from enforcement. State what the evidence does not establish.</p><p><a href="/semantic-modelling/method/scope-and-package">Eight concerns, six outputs →</a></p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="misreading"><p>Publishing a vocabulary is not permission to publish personal records. A complete metadata record is not proof that a claim is true, and passing technical checks does not adopt a standard.</p></Fragment>
  </PrincipleSpread>

  <h2 id="tradeoffs"><span id="evolve" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="success" aria-hidden="true"></span>The trade-off we choose openly</h2>
  <ContrastPair leftLabel="What this approach protects" rightLabel="What it asks us to maintain" accent="none">
    <Fragment slot="left"><p>Local distinctions remain reviewable. Change has an accountable home. Shared meanings can be reused without forcing all contexts into one vocabulary. Implementers can inspect the assumptions behind a connection.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="right"><p>Boundaries and mappings require stewardship. Local autonomy can produce duplication unless reuse is actively reviewed. A common term still needs a release and change agreement. These are ongoing responsibilities, not costs that linked data makes disappear.</p></Fragment>
  </ContrastPair>
  <p>
    We are not claiming an industry-wide consensus that all ontology work follows one
    top-down pattern. Top-down and bottom-up techniques can both contribute. Our
    commitment is to begin with evidenced local questions, retain formal discipline
    and make the step from local understanding to shared agreement explicit.
  </p>

  <h2 id="source-contract"><span id="authority" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="receipt" aria-hidden="true"></span>What makes this a method rather than a slogan</h2>
  <p>
    Source-method ODR-0071 supplies the selected concerns; ODR-0015/0040/0106 the
    boundary discipline; ODR-0025/0026 and ODR-0112/0115/0118 the identity and analytical
    commitments; ODR-0014/0030/0036 the applicability and language contracts; and
    ODR-0087/0096/0098/0127 the mapping and context-map rules. The explanatory manifesto
    above synthesises those requirements—it does not create additional adopted rules.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/standards-and-decisions">decision register</a>
    pins the reviewed source revision, OPDA scope and relevant amendments. It separates
    normative method, proposed operating workflow and current candidate evidence.
    The external standards remain authoritative for the semantics of their terms.
  </p>
  <ChapterEnd closing="Start small enough to inspect, but connect the meaning all the way through." nextHref="/semantic-modelling/method/from-question-to-candidate" nextTitle="From question to candidate" nextDescription="Work through a modelling cycle, including counterexamples, review and the limits of automated checks." related={[
    { href: '/semantic-modelling/method/languages-and-profiles', label: 'Languages and profiles' },
    { href: '/semantic-modelling/contribute', label: 'The participant route' },
  ]} />
</ModellingLayout>
